  introDialogue: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c454d2cbd878f6c43a1186adcbc49f01, type: 2}
  questionResponses:
  - question: Where are you coming from?
    earlyDaysAnswer: The back room of the Metropol. Cards until dawn. Now home to the harbor flats to sleep it off.
    midDaysAnswer: The Metropol shut when the curfew started. There is a basement game near the tram depot. Low stakes, bad light.
    finalDaysAnswer: A cellar across the river. Last game in town, three players and one candle. I folded early. Back to my bunk now.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: Off the evening train from Kutaisi. First time in your town, officer. They tell me the Metropol runs a good game.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: My shift at the port, loading freighters. Honest work now, officer. I gave up the cards years ago.
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: The Metropol. They opened the card room again, full tables all night. Best game I have seen in months.
    uncannyEarlyDaysAnswer: 
    uncannyMidDaysAnswer: 
    uncannyFinalDaysAnswer: 
    cameraTrigger: 
  - question: Are you experiencing any symptoms?
    earlyDaysAnswer: Sweaty palms, same as always. Bad for holding cards, good for bluffing. Otherwise nothing.
    midDaysAnswer: I sleep all afternoon and still wake up tired. Headaches when the numbers will not sit still. Nothing the polyclinic can fix.
    finalDaysAnswer: I forget hands now. Whole hands. Twenty years I never forgot a card. Write that down however you like.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: Nothing. Dry hands, steady as a surgeon. Always been that way, even at the table.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: Never better. No late nights since I quit the cards, so no headaches either. I sleep like a Party official.
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: Nothing. At forty-five a man learns to pace himself, officer. I rest, I eat, I stay out of trouble.
    uncannyEarlyDaysAnswer: 
    uncannyMidDaysAnswer: 
    uncannyFinalDaysAnswer: 
    cameraTrigger: 
  idleBarks:
    earlyDays:
    - Heads or tails, officer? Just kidding. I know when the odds are against me.
    - You have a poker face that would bankrupt me in ten minutes. I mean that as a compliment.
    - I put everything on a quick recovery for the city. The spread looked good at the time.
    midDays:
    - The house always wins, does it not? And right now the house is on fire.
    - I found a four-leaf clover in the ash this morning. Taking that as a sign.
    - The odds keep shifting. I keep adjusting. That is all you can do.
    finalDays:
    - I am going home to fold my hand. There is no out-bluffing the end of the world.
    - Luck is a lady but she left on the last bus out.
    - Every player knows when to walk away from the table. I am almost there.
    uncannyEarlyDays: []
    uncannyMidDays: []
    uncannyFinalDays: []
